layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | By the end of this page you will have a password, a paired computer, a Claude Code session showing up live on the
    strong Floor
    | , and notifications on your phone. Plan for about fifteen minutes. You do not need to understand how anything
    | works yet;
    a(href="/docs/concepts") How it works
    | explains that afterwards.

  h2#before-you-start Before you start
  p You need three things:
  ul
    li
      strong A running Buildyboy server.
      | If you are reading this page on your own server, that part is done: it lives at
      code {{ site.url }}
      | . If not, follow
      a(href="/docs/server") Running the server
      | first and come back.
    li
      strong A computer where you use Claude Code
      | (Windows, WSL, macOS or Linux) with Node.js installed. The connection pieces are small Node scripts.
    li
      strong Your phone,
      | if you want approvals and notifications away from your desk. This is optional but it is the point of walk mode.

  h2#step-1 Step 1 · Choose your password
  p
    | The very first time the server starts it has no password. It prints a one-time setup link in its log, which looks
    | like this:
  pre
    code
      | [buildyboy] no password set. Open {{ site.url }}/setup?token=Xq3…  (or run: bb set-password)
  ol
    li
      | Find the link. On the standard WSL install, run
      code journalctl -u buildyboy | grep setup
      | inside WSL. The deploy script also prints it at the end of a first deploy.
    li Open the link in your browser. You see a small form titled with the Buildyboy logo and the words "Choose a password to finish setup".
    li
      | Type a password of
      strong at least 8 characters
      | into both boxes and press
      strong Set password
      | . You are logged in and taken to the Floor.
  p
    | Lost the link? It stays valid until a password is set, and survives restarts, so the same
    code journalctl
    | command finds it again. You can also set the password from the server's shell instead; see
    a(href="/docs/commands#set-password") bb set-password
    | .
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="login-phone.png" alt="The Buildyboy login form on a phone: the logo, a password field and a Log in button." caption="The login screen. Later visits ask only for the password." phone)
  p
    | Your login lasts 30 days per browser. After that, or after you log out, you see the same form with a single
    strong Password
    | field and a
    strong Log in
    | button.

  h2#step-2 Step 2 · Put it on your phone's home screen
  p
    | Buildyboy is a web app that can be installed like a phone app (a "PWA"). Installing it gives you a full-screen
    | window and, on iPhone, is
    strong required
    | for push notifications.
  ul
    li
      strong iPhone and iPad (Safari):
      | open
      code {{ site.url }}
      | , tap the Share button, then
      strong Add to Home Screen
      | .
    li
      strong Android (Chrome):
      | open the address, tap the ⋮ menu, then
      strong Add to Home screen
      | or
      strong Install app
      | .
  p Log in once inside the installed app; it keeps its own login.

  h2#step-3 Step 3 · Pair your first computer
  p
    | Pairing installs a small plugin into Claude Code on that computer and gives the computer its own access key. You
    | do it once per computer.
  ol
    li
      | In Buildyboy, open
      strong Machines
      | (in the top bar on a computer; on a phone, use the address
      code {{ site.url }}/machines
      | ) and press
      strong Pair a machine
      | .
    li
      | Optionally type a name such as
      code work-laptop
      | , then press
      strong Create pairing code
      | . An 8-character code appears with a countdown. It works
      strong once
      | and expires after
      strong 10 minutes
      | .
    li
      | Copy the command for your system with its
      strong Copy
      | button and run it in a terminal on the computer you are pairing.
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="machines-pair-desktop.png" alt="The Pair a machine panel: a large pairing code, an expiry countdown, and one install command for Linux, macOS and WSL and one for Windows PowerShell." caption="Machines → Pair a machine. Each command already contains the code and your server's address.")
  p The two commands look like this, with your code in place of PAIRCODE:
  p
    strong Linux, macOS and WSL
  pre
    code
      | curl -fsSL {{ site.url }}/install.sh | bash -s -- PAIRCODE
  p
    strong Windows (PowerShell)
  pre
    code
      | $env:BB_CODE='PAIRCODE'; irm {{ site.url }}/install.ps1 | iex
  p
    | The installer prints what it does and ends with "Buildyboy is installed on …". It never edits your Claude Code
    | settings file.
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        strong Windows and WSL are two different computers here.
        | Each keeps its own Claude Code settings, so if you use Claude Code in both, pair both, each with a fresh code.
        | Give each a name (for example
        code laptop
        | and
        code laptop-wsl
        | ) so you can tell them apart; otherwise both may get the same name.

  h2#step-4 Step 4 · Start Claude Code and watch it appear
  ol
    li
      | Open a
      strong new
      | terminal on the paired computer (sessions that were already running do not have the plugin) and go to any
      | project folder.
    li
      | Start Claude Code as usual with
      code claude
      | .
    li
      | Type
      code /bb-status
      | and press Enter. It should say
      code Server: … (reachable)
      | and show the name of this machine.
    li
      | Go back to Buildyboy's
      strong Floor
      | . Within a second your session appears as a card on your machine's bay.
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="floor-desktop.png" alt="The Floor on a desktop: two machine bays with session cards; three cards glow amber and say Waiting on you, one is blue and Idle, one is grey and Ended." caption="The Floor. Each dashed box is a computer; each card is a Claude Code session.")
  p
    | If nothing shows up, the
    a(href="/docs/troubleshooting#session-missing") troubleshooting page
    | walks through the usual causes.

  h2#step-5 Step 5 · Turn on notifications
  p Do this on your phone (inside the installed app) and on any computer where you want desktop notifications.
  ol
    li
      | Open
      strong Settings
      | and find the
      strong Notifications
      | section.
    li
      | Check the device name (it is pre-filled, for example "Chrome on Android") and press
      strong Enable notifications on this device
      | . Your browser asks for permission; allow it.
    li
      | Press
      strong Send test notification
      | . A notification titled "Buildyboy test notification" should arrive within a few seconds.
  p
    | Notifications do not arrive?
    a(href="/docs/notifications") Notifications
    | covers iPhone limits, blocked permissions and the
    strong ntfy
    | fallback.

  h2#step-6 Step 6 · Try walk mode
  p
    | Walk mode is the reason Buildyboy exists. Normally Claude Code asks for permission in your terminal and waits for you
    | there. With walk mode on, the same question goes to your phone instead, and you answer it with one tap.
  ol
    li
      | Open the
      strong Inbox
      | and switch on
      strong Walk mode
      | .
    li
      | In your Claude Code session, ask for something that needs permission, for example "create a file called
      | hello.txt".
    li
      | Your phone shows a notification. Tap it, or open the Inbox, and press
      strong Allow once
      | . Claude continues immediately.
    li
      | Back at your desk, switch
      strong Walk mode
      | off again, so every finished turn does not wait for a reply.
  p
    | The full story, including what happens if you do not answer in time, is in
    a(href="/docs/walk-mode") Walk mode
    | .

  h2#next Where to go next
  ul
    li
      a(href="/docs/concepts") How it works
      | : the handful of ideas behind everything else. Worth five minutes.
    li
      a(href="/docs/floor") The Floor
      | and
      a(href="/docs/sessions") Following a session
      | : reading what your sessions are doing.
    li
      a(href="/docs/composer") The Composer
      | and
      a(href="/docs/jobs") Jobs
      | : starting unattended work from your phone (needs the
      a(href="/docs/runner") runner
      | ).
